# A Gene and Its Centromere

@left

![Centromere joining sister chromatids](assets/lect09_tetrad_concepts/centromere.drawn.svg)

@right

- The centromere is the reference point.
- Crossing over between the gene and centromere can change when its alleles separate.
- Find the distance from gene A to its centromere.

=== layout: definitions

# Read the Segregation Patterns

@body

![First- and second-division pattern shapes](assets/lect09c-tetrad_worked_examples_bpw/ordered_classes.svg)

- [*First-Division Segregation (FDS)*]{color=#076DAD}: alleles separate at meiosis I; 4:4.
- [*Second-Division Segregation (SDS)*]{color=#B74300}: alleles separate at meiosis II; 2:2:2:2 or 2:4:2.

@notes

Read each ascus from one end to the other. The numbers describe consecutive blocks of the same allele, not the total number of plus and a spores. Turning an ascus around preserves its class. These patterns assume regular 4:4 allele segregation. The distance calculation uses the single-crossover model for the gene-centromere interval.

